Z||000003||Monkeyman||10-19-2000||09:31 PM||||hugh--That sounds good, except that's just for the motor (I assume).  That wouldn't include the tranny or any labor.  Thanx anyway.  <P>Phil--$125 each????  Wow!  I had no idea they cost that much.  I guess I had better rethink my exhaust.  Something fairly cheap with a nice sound and good looking exhaust tips.||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||10-19-2000||10:56 PM||||MM, you need to figure out what you want and then build (or buy) the motor that best suits your desires, within your allotted budget of course. Maybe I'm wrong, but you don't seem to be the gung-ho hotrodder type. But, you are a Fiero lover, so you do enjoy better than average performance and handling, that's obvious. I'm guessing you want a good engine that has some modest power gains from what you currently have, but you also want it to be reliable and affordable. To be frank, that probably leaves out any of the engine swaps.<P>For your budget and needs, you're probably well off to rebuild what you have, assuming it's a solid rebuildable engine. Stroking it to 3.1 would be nice as a cam change would be in order too. Like an aftermarket Crane CompuCam or one of those GM hipo cams. If you splurge a little on headers (or ported manifolds) and a good flowing exhaust system, I think you will be pleased. You should get at least a 25-30 hp increase over stock with this combo I described and lots more torque too. The best of both worlds. Just some of my ramblings, feel free to ignore any or all of it.||||reg||1
